+\kevin{\medskip\noindent\emph{Direct particle-MSE calculation.}\quad
+We now prove Equations~(\ref{eq:review-alpha-particle-mse}) and
+(\ref{eq:review-alpha-particle-bias}), retaining the dependence between
+all creation times before taking second moments. The original variance
+argument continues below this calculation. We distinguish the two
+estimators by $\nabla_\theta\hat\ell_J^{A,\alpha}$ and
+$\nabla_\theta\hat\ell_J^{B,\alpha}$, and put
+$H_N(\alpha)=\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}\alpha^q$.
+\endgraf
+\noindent\textit{1. Sum the discounted endpoints.}
+We first prove the result for the after-resampling score. The usual
+weight-gradient recursion and a finite telescope give
+\begin{align}
+\nabla_\theta\hat\ell_J^{A,\alpha}
+&=\sum_{s=1}^N\sum_{b=s}^N c_b\alpha^{b-s}m(\xi_b)(h_s),
+&c_b&=\begin{cases}1-\alpha,&b<N,\\1,&b=N.\end{cases}
+\label{eq:logfree-endpoints}
+\end{align}
+Here $h_s$ is the time-$s$ measurement gradient, evaluated along the
+relevant ancestral path, as in Equation~(\ref{eq:actual-path-score}). Replacing $m(\xi_b)$ by
+$\rho_b$ gives $\nabla_\theta\ell^\alpha$. For each creation time $s$,
+\begin{align}
+\sum_{b=s}^N c_b\alpha^{b-s}&=1,\notag\\
+\sum_{b=s}^N c_b\alpha^{b-s}(b-s+1)
+&=\sum_{q=0}^{N-s}\sum_{b=s+q}^N c_b\alpha^{b-s}
+=\sum_{q=0}^{N-s}\alpha^q
+\leq H_N(\alpha).
+\label{eq:logfree-endpoint-moments}
+\end{align}
+These are polynomial identities, including both endpoints $\alpha=0,1$.
+\endgraf
+Fix one coordinate $i$. Under (A6), use Equation~(\ref{eq:measurement-gradient-age-sum})
+\[
+h_{s,i}=\sum_{r=0}^s h_{s,i}^{[r]},\qquad
+\|h_{s,i}^{[r]}\|_\infty\leq CG'(\theta)\kappa^r.
+\]
+For a fixed age $r$, set $a_s=\max(1,s-r-1)$ and set the component to
+zero when $r>s$. It depends on the physical block from $a_s$ to $s$
+and its simulator draws, including the initial history when necessary.
+Under (A6-J), use the joint state, $a_s=s$, and only $r=0$.
+Let $Z_r$ be the coordinate-$i$, age-$r$ part of \eqref{eq:logfree-endpoints},
+and let $z_r$ be its population counterpart.
+\endgraf
+\noindent\textit{2. Condition on prediction clouds.}
+Under (A6), condition on the prediction states alone:
+\[
+\mathcal C=\sigma(X_{u,j}^{P,\phi}:1\leq u\leq N+1,\ 1\leq j\leq J).
+\]
+Under (A6-J), replace each state by its joint state and gradient.
+The last transition is to a one-point space, with no additional observation.
+Its parent indices record the actual time-$N$ resampling, but neither
+parent indices nor simulator draws are included in $\mathcal C$.
+For $2\leq u\leq N$,
+conditioning on these clouds gives the parent probabilities
+\begin{equation}
+B_u^J(j,l)=
+\frac{g_{u-1,l}^{\phi}
+ f_{X_u\mid X_{u-1}}(X_{u,j}^{P,\phi}\mid X_{u-1,l}^{P,\phi};\phi)}
+{\sum_vg_{u-1,v}^{\phi}
+ f_{X_u\mid X_{u-1}}(X_{u,j}^{P,\phi}\mid X_{u-1,v}^{P,\phi};\phi)}.
+\label{eq:logfree-backward}
+\end{equation}
+Under (A6-J), use the joint transition density. At the artificial last
+layer, the transition density is one, and hence
+\[
+B_{N+1}^J(j,l)=g_{N,l}^{\phi}/\sum_v g_{N,v}^{\phi}.
+\]
+To see the conditional independence explicitly, let $K_{u-1,j}$ be
+the parent of prediction node $(u,j)$, and let $R_u(x,x';d\omega)$
+be the conditional law of its simulator draw given its incoming and
+outgoing states. Bayes' rule gives
+\begin{align*}
+&\Pr\{K_{u-1,j}=l_{u,j},\ \omega_{u,j}\in d\omega_{u,j}
+                 \text{ for all }u\geq2,j\mid\mathcal C\}\\
+&\qquad=\prod_{u=2}^{N+1}\prod_{j=1}^J
+ B_u^J(j,l_{u,j})
+ R_u(X_{u-1,l_{u,j}}^{P,\phi},X_{u,j}^{P,\phi};d\omega_{u,j}).
+\end{align*}
+At $N+1$ the draw is a deterministic dummy variable. Under (A6-J),
+there is no need to retain simulator draws. The initial-history bridges
+also factor over first prediction nodes. Thus distinct nodes have
+independent parent choices and conditional simulator draws, even when
+they choose the same parent. A shared node has one shared parent and draw.
+The stated density bounds give
+\begin{equation}
+\max_{j,l}B_u^J(j,l)\leq C/J,\qquad
+\operatorname{osc}(B_u^Jf)\leq\varrho_*\operatorname{osc}(f),\qquad 0\leq\varrho_*<1.
+\label{eq:logfree-backward-bounds}
+\end{equation}
+Choose $\varrho_*$ to dominate both the backward contraction coefficient
+and the exact forward conditional contraction coefficient, using the transition density bounds.
+Both are uniformly below one by the transition density bounds.
+It is distinct from $\varrho$ in the population-bias bound.
+\endgraf
+Integrating the simulator draws conditional on adjacent physical states
+turns $h_{s,i}^{[r]}$ into a deterministic bounded block function
+$f_{s,r}$. This
+integration is a model kernel, not a random function of future clouds.
+At time one, integrate the initial history conditional on the first state.
+The conditional expectation of $m(\xi_b)(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$ is the backward
+smoother with uniform terminal weights at $b+1$. Its population target
+is $\rho_b(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$, since this last transition has no observation.
+\endgraf
+\noindent\textit{3. Sum the predictable local errors before taking norms.}
+Use the growing prediction-cloud filtration $\mathcal H_u$, with
+$\mathcal H_0$ trivial. Write $\mu_u$ for the conditional prediction
+law given $\mathcal H_{u-1}$ and $\eta_u^J$ for its empirical measure.
+The prediction draws are conditionally iid. The calculation in this
+step is under the ordinary particle law, not conditional on the full
+$\mathcal C$.
+\endgraf
+We write out the finite-particle telescope from Definition 5.1 and
+equation (7.1) of \citet{delmoral10backward}. Fix $(s,b,r)$ and
+write $T=b+1$. Let $M_{v+1}$ be the exact transition kernel and
+$g_v(x)=f_{Y_v\mid X_v}(y_v^*\mid x;\phi)$. The empirical backward
+kernel $B_v^J(x_v,dx_{v-1})$ uses the prediction cloud at $v-1$ and
+the same density ratio as \eqref{eq:logfree-backward}. Define
+\begin{align*}
+D_{u,T}^Jf(x_u)
+&=\int f(x_{1:T})
+ \prod_{v=2}^{u}B_v^J(x_v,dx_{v-1})
+ \prod_{v=u}^{T-1}g_v(x_v)M_{v+1}(x_v,dx_{v+1}),\\
+Q_{u,T}1(x_u)&=D_{u,T}^J1(x_u),\qquad
+A_u=\frac{\eta_u^J(D_{u,T}^Jf_{s,r})}{\eta_u^J(Q_{u,T}1)}.
+\end{align*}
+Empty products are one. The initial-history bridge has already been
+integrated into $f_{s,r}$. For $u\geq2$, the conditional prediction law
+and Bayes' formula for $B_u^J$ give the measure identity
+\[
+\mu_u(dx_u)B_u^J(x_u,dx_{u-1})
+=\frac{\eta_{u-1}^J(dx_{u-1})g_{u-1}(x_{u-1})
+                 M_u(x_{u-1},dx_u)}{\eta_{u-1}^J(g_{u-1})}.
+\]
+Integrating the remaining factors on both sides yields
+\begin{align*}
+\mu_u(D_{u,T}^Jf_{s,r})
+&=\frac{\eta_{u-1}^J(D_{u-1,T}^Jf_{s,r})}
+             {\eta_{u-1}^J(g_{u-1})},\\
+\mu_u(Q_{u,T}1)
+&=\frac{\eta_{u-1}^J(Q_{u-1,T}1)}{\eta_{u-1}^J(g_{u-1})}.
+\end{align*}
+Consequently, their ratio is $A_{u-1}$. For $u=1$, the corresponding
+ratio uses the exact initial prediction law and equals
+$A_0:=\rho_b(h_{s,i}^{[r]})$. At $u=T$, $Q_{T,T}1=1$ and the
+empirical backward integral is the conditional expectation from Step 2.
+Thus $e_{u;s,b}:=A_u-A_{u-1}$ satisfies the exact finite telescope
+\begin{equation}
+\E[m(\xi_b)(h_{s,i}^{[r]})\mid\mathcal C]
+ -\rho_b(h_{s,i}^{[r]})=\sum_{u=1}^{b+1}e_{u;s,b}.
+\label{eq:logfree-dds}
+\end{equation}
+For its ratio expansion, put
+\begin{align}
+q_{u;b}&=\frac{Q_{u,b+1}1}{\mu_u(Q_{u,b+1}1)},
+&F_{u;s,b}&=\frac{D_{u,b+1}^Jf_{s,r}}{Q_{u,b+1}1},\notag\\
+d_{u;s,b}&=q_{u;b}\{F_{u;s,b}-\mu_u(q_{u;b}F_{u;s,b})\}.
+\end{align}
+All these functions are $\mathcal H_{u-1}$-measurable. The exact ratio
+expansion is
+\begin{align}
+\mu_uq_{u;b}&=1,&\mu_ud_{u;s,b}&=0,\notag\\
+e_{u;s,b}
+&=(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_{u;s,b}
+-\frac{\{(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_{u;s,b}\}
+        \{(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)q_{u;b}\}}{\eta_u^Jq_{u;b}}.
+\label{eq:logfree-ratio}
+\end{align}
+The forward and backward contractions, applied on either side of the
+block $[a_s,s]$, give the following bounds:
+\begin{equation}
+C^{-1}\leq q_{u;b}\leq C,\qquad
+\operatorname{osc}(d_{u;s,b})\leq CG'(\theta)\kappa^r
+ \varrho_*^{\operatorname{dist}(u,[a_s,s])}.
+\label{eq:logfree-local-bounds}
+\end{equation}
+Before $a_s$, the exact forward conditional kernels contract up to the
+block. After $s$, the empirical backward kernels contract down to it.
+Inside the block, boundedness suffices. The terminal potential at $b+1$
+is one and does not change the common prediction law $\mu_{b+1}$.
+At the artificial last cloud, $\eta_{N+1}^J=\mu_{N+1}$, so its local
+forward error is zero.
+\endgraf
+For the linear terms in \eqref{eq:logfree-ratio}, define the predictable sum
+\[
+d_u^{[r]}=\sum_{s=1}^N\sum_{b=\max(s,u-1)}^N
+ c_b\alpha^{b-s}d_{u;s,b}.
+\]
+The endpoint weights in each inner sum have total mass at most one.
+Also, there are at most $r+2$ blocks containing a fixed time $u$, with
+geometric tails on each side. Consequently,
+\begin{align}
+\sum_{s=1}^N\varrho_*^{\operatorname{dist}(u,[a_s,s])}
+&\leq r+2+\frac{2}{1-\varrho_*}\leq C(r+2),\notag\\
+\operatorname{osc}(d_u^{[r]})&\leq CG'(\theta)\kappa^r(r+2).
+\end{align}
+Set $L_u=(\eta_u^J-\mu_u)d_u^{[r]}$. Conditional iid sampling yields
